== Course Overview
ECE 605 is an advanced graduate research seminar and project course dedicated to the scientific principles, systems architectures, and learning dynamics of frontier Large-Scale AI. 

The Fall 2026 offering focuses on the convergence of *Vision-Language Models, Generative Action Policies, and Embodied AI*, exploring five thematic clusters through breakthrough literature:
- *Challenge Foundations & Interactive Simulation*: Deep dive into the OmniGibson environment, BEHAVIOR-1K benchmark, and state-of-the-art competition solutions.
- *Vision-Language-Action (VLA) Architectures*: From multimodal backbones (ViT, SigLIP, PaliGemma) to continuous flow-based policies ($\pi_0$, $\pi_{0.5}$, $\pi_{RL}$), generalist humanoids (GR00T, Octo, OpenVLA), and System 2 action reasoning (MolmoAct2).
- *Robotics Data Systems & Scaling Laws*: Cross-embodiment agglomeration datasets (Open X-Embodiment, DROID), novel demonstration pipelines (UMI, DART), synthetic simulation data (RoboGen), and empirical data scaling laws.
- *World Models & Latent Planning*: Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (I-JEPA, V-JEPA 2.1, LeWorldModel) and generative video foundation models as physics engines for real-time planning.
- *Hybrid & Multimodal Frontiers*: Distilling classical motion planners into visual policies and integrating tactile/force feedback via Mixture-of-Experts (ForceVLA).

== Class schedule
- Monday and Wednesday, 10:30-11:45, Moore Hall 203 ([https://hawaii.zoom.us/j/84384222329 zoom link]; password: ece)
- Office hours: by [https://calendar.app.google/mLp9CvS96utpEjDK6 appointment] or after class

== Prerequisites
- *Essential background*: [https://igormolybog.github.io/ece405_sprg2026.html ECE405 Introduction to Large-Scale AI Systems] (undergraduate version). Mastery of the material covered in ECE405 (or equivalent) is crucial for success in ECE605. If you have not taken it, please review the ECE405 lecture slides, recordings, and assignments.

